      "writtenOn": "2026-10-13T22:51:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Two questions on the sensor state after `fp_finger_status()` / `fp_process_finger()` when `FP_MODE_FINGER_UP` remains set (`st !\u003d FINGER_NONE`):\n\n1. Does `fp_configure_detect()` need to be called again when `FP_MODE_FINGER_UP` is still active after a poll (or if `FP_MODE_ANY_CAPTURE | FP_MODE_FINGER_UP` were set together and `fp_process_finger()` just captured an image and cleared `FP_MODE_ANY_CAPTURE`)? Several sensor libraries either state that `fp_sensor_finger_status()` assumes `fp_sensor_configure_detect()` was called before each check (e.g. `fingerprint_fpc1025_private.h`, `fingerprint_egis660_private.h`), or stop detect mode inside `fp_finger_status()` (e.g. `isensor_read_interrupt_type_ec630()` calls `ec630_stop_detect_mode()` when reading an FOD interrupt).\n2. For b/560263098 and b/565886719 (`egis630`), `egis630_finger_status()` -\u003e `egis_check_int_status()` -\u003e `convert_sensor_interrupt_type_to_result()` maps both `SENSOR_INTERRUPT_THL` and `SENSOR_INTERRUPT_NONE` (`0x80`) to `EGIS_API_FINGER_LOST` (`FINGER_NONE`). If the user holds their finger on the sensor for \u003e\u003d 100ms so `THL` hasn\u0027t triggered when `TASK_EVENT_TIMER` fires, won\u0027t `fp_finger_status()` still read `SENSOR_INTERRUPT_NONE` and report `FINGER_NONE` prematurely unless the Egis driver/library is also updated?",
