      "writtenOn": "2026-10-13T22:51:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why call `fp_idle()` (`FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_IDLE`) immediately before `fp_sensor_low_power()` (`FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_LOW_POWER`)?\n\nAs noted in the commit message, `FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_IDLE` is defined as \"The sensor is waiting for requests\", so transitioning to `IDLE` and immediately to `LOW_POWER` on the next line seems contradictory:\n- In 5 of the 6 Zephyr drivers (`fpc1025`, `egis630`, `egis660`, `elan_series`, `ft98xx`), `FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_LOW_POWER` already disables the GPIO IRQ.\n- On `ft98xx`, `FOCAL_SENSOR_MODE_IDLE` and `FOCAL_SENSOR_MODE_LOW_POWER` both allocate a 20KB buffer and send SPI commands to enter `FF_POWER_MODE_DEEP_SLEEP`, so calling `fp_idle()` followed by `fp_sensor_low_power()` runs that sequence twice back-to-back.\n- The only driver where `FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_LOW_POWER` doesn\u0027t disable the IRQ is `fpc1145` (where `LOW_POWER` is a `break;` stub). Should `fpc1145_set_mode()` disable the IRQ in `FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_LOW_POWER` like the other drivers instead of calling `fp_idle()` before `fp_sensor_low_power()` here?",
